wasi-sh: compgen — busybox's own completion, callable without its editor.

Applied LAST, and it depends on two earlier patches: ash-hostbuiltin.patch,
whose registry is one of the kinds of name a command can be here, and
ash-shebang.patch, whose `env` builtin already solved this file's one real
hazard (see hunk 3) and is the model for how hunk 3 solves it again.

WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. libbb/lineedit.c already knows how to complete a
word — applet names, this shell's builtins, its functions, its aliases, a
PATH scan, directory-vs-file, and an ESC-injection filter on every candidate.
All of it is reachable only from input_tab(), which runs only while the guest
is drawing its own prompt.

That is one of the two ways a terminal gets built on this shell, and it is
not the common one. Both examples/repl.html and the wide IDE edit lines in
the PAGE and hand the guest a finished command. wide has no choice: its guest
is parked on a /dev/hostreq verb loop answering an iframe's fetches, and a
guest parked at a prompt is parked on stdin and answers nothing until Enter.
So the engine is exposed twice rather than written twice.

  compgen -c WORD    command names          (FIND_EXE_ONLY)
  compgen -f WORD    files and directories   (FIND_FILE_ONLY)
  compgen -d WORD    directories only        (FIND_DIR_ONLY)

One candidate per line on stdout, status 1 when there are none — bash's
compgen reports the same. Newline-separated output is unambiguous for free:
add_match() already drops any candidate containing a control character.

Two inherited properties callers have to know. Neither is "fixed" here,
because the guest's own Tab behaves this way and two completions that
disagreed would be worse than one that surprises:

  - candidates are BASENAMES, not paths — `compgen -f src/te` answers
    `terminal.mjs`, and the caller re-attaches `src/`;
  - a directory carries a trailing slash; a file does not.

HUNK 1 (libbb/lineedit.c) hoists input_tab()'s sort-and-dedup into a function
and exports one entry point beside it.

The dedup is not a detail: `echo` is both a busybox applet and a shell
builtin, so the raw engine answers it twice, and `compgen -c ec` printed
"echo\necho" until the two shared this step. A completion list that differs
between the guest's Tab and a host's Tab is the one outcome this patch is
built to prevent, so the code is hoisted and called twice rather than copied.

complete_cmd_dir_file() is static and writes S.matches/S.num_matches, where S
is the statics block INIT_S() allocates per read_line_input() call, and the
flags it reads come through the file-scope `state`. Neither exists outside a
live prompt, so the wrapper owns an INIT_S()/DEINIT_S() pair of its own and
sets `state` itself. That is safe rather than reentrant: the caller is a
builtin, and a shell that does one thing at a time cannot be editing a line
while it runs one. S's pointer to the vector is cleared before DEINIT_S() so
the caller's free is the only one.

What it does NOT copy from read_line_input() is the `&const_int_0` stand-in
for a missing state. That is a four-byte object playing a whole line_input_t,
and it is sound up there only because input_tab() bails on
`flags & TAB_COMPLETION` before reading anything past `flags`. There is no
such gate down here: complete_cmd_dir_file() reads state->path_lookup and
indirect-calls state->get_exe_name, both well past those four bytes. A NULL
state answers zero matches instead.

HUNK 2 (include/libbb.h) declares it beside read_line_input(), with names for
lineedit.c's private FIND_* values.

HUNK 3 (shell/ash.c) is compgencmd() and its builtintab entry.

  *** The hazard is the table, not the function. *** builtintab is bsearch'd
  and must stay sorted, which puts "compgen" between "command" and
  "continue" — and COMMANDCMD/ENVCMD/EVALCMD/EXECCMD are HAND-COUNTED offsets
  into that same table, accumulated entry by entry across its #if ladder. An
  entry inserted after "command" without a matching term in ENVCMD's sum
  silently repoints `env`, `eval` and `exec` at their neighbours, and nothing
  warns. So the entry ships with `1 * ENABLE_FEATURE_TAB_COMPLETION` in that
  sum — the same move ash-shebang.patch made when it added `env`, and for the
  same reason.

  The other thing worth naming: line_input_state exists only for an
  INTERACTIVE shell, and the consumers this builtin is for are never
  interactive. The non-interactive path therefore builds a line_input_t of
  its own rather than dereferencing NULL, and both paths refresh path_lookup
  from pathval() first, because a `cd` and a PATH assignment can both land
  between two completions.

HUNK 4 (shell/ash.c) teaches ash_command_name() about host builtins.

That is upstream's enumerator for completion — builtins, then functions, then
aliases — so extending it is what keeps `compgen -c` and the guest's own Tab
agreeing. Host builtins are the fourth kind of name find_command() resolves
in this build, and the only kind a host cannot enumerate for itself: the
registry lives in JS behind a lookup(name) that answers one question at a
time. Without this arm, `php` is a command the shell runs and completion has
never heard of.

The far end is optional. The JS contract's names() is opt-in, because an
embedder may register a lazy namespace with nothing to list; then
host_builtin_name(0) is NULL and the list simply ends, exactly as it does in
a build with no host builtins at all.

--- a/libbb/lineedit.c
+++ b/libbb/lineedit.c
